---
title: SvelteKit library
description: The GateKeeper SvelteKit BFF package: sessions, refresh, user data, and MFA UI APIs.
section: Application libraries
---

`@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff` is the SvelteKit integration package for GateKeeper. It packages the
client API that used to live inside the example frontend into a reusable TypeScript library that a
SvelteKit application can install and configure.

The package source lives at `packages/gatekeeper-sveltekit-bff`. The current frontend uses it as a
working example.

## What it provides

The package covers the pieces a SvelteKit app needs to act as a secure BFF:

- OAuth 2.1 Authorization Code + PKCE helpers.
- Encrypted `httpOnly` session cookies.
- `hooks.server.ts` integration that loads `event.locals.session`.
- Automatic access-token refresh when the token is near expiry.
- Refresh-token rotation coalescing, so concurrent requests do not reuse the same refresh token.
- Server-side helpers for `getSession`, `requireSession`, `getUser`, `getAccessToken`,
  `getIdToken`, and `getRefreshToken`.
- A browser-safe session view for layouts and components.
- A Svelte provider that keeps browser session state fresh without exposing tokens.
- Typed profile update helpers for `PUT /me/profile`.
- MFA configuration APIs, including a default Svelte component and headless functions.

## Configure the client

Create one server-side client from your private environment variables:

```ts
import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private';
import { dev } from '$app/environment';
import { createSvelteKitGateKeeperClient } from '@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff';

export const gatekeeper = createSvelteKitGateKeeperClient({
	idpBaseUrl: env.IDP_BASE_URL,
	authUiBaseUrl: env.AUTH_UI_BASE_URL,
	tenantId: env.OAUTH_TENANT_ID,
	clientId: env.OAUTH_CLIENT_ID,
	clientSecret: env.OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET,
	redirectUri: env.OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI,
	scope: env.OAUTH_SCOPE || 'openid profile email',
	sessionSecret: env.SESSION_SECRET,
	secureCookies: !dev
});
```

`SESSION_SECRET` protects the encrypted session cookie. Use a strong production secret and rotate it
as operational key material, because changing it invalidates existing sessions.

## Load and refresh sessions

Use the SvelteKit facade in `hooks.server.ts`:

```ts
import { gatekeeper } from '$lib/server/gatekeeper';
import type { Handle } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const handle: Handle = ({ event, resolve }) =>
	gatekeeper.handle({
		event,
		resolve: (resolvedEvent) => resolve(resolvedEvent as typeof event)
	});
```

On every request, `handle()` reads the encrypted session cookie, refreshes the access token when it
is close to expiry, writes the rotated session cookie, stores the result in `event.locals.session`,
and applies basic hardening headers.

By default refresh starts when the token has 120 seconds or less remaining. Pass
`{ refreshSkewSeconds: 300 }` as the second argument to `createSvelteKitGateKeeperClient()` if your
app needs a wider window.

## Read user data on the server

Server routes, actions, and load functions can read the current session from `event.locals`:

```ts
const session = gatekeeper.getSession(event);
const user = gatekeeper.getUser(event);
const accessToken = gatekeeper.getAccessToken(event);
const idToken = gatekeeper.getIdToken(event);
const refreshToken = gatekeeper.getRefreshToken(event);
```

Use `requireSession(event)` when the page or endpoint must be signed in. It throws
`GateKeeperSessionRequiredError` if there is no active session.

Keep token access server-side. Browser code should receive only the browser-safe view:

```ts
export const load = ({ locals }) => {
	const session = gatekeeper.getBrowserSession(locals);
	return { gatekeeper: session, user: session.user };
};
```

That object contains `authenticated`, `user`, and `expiresAt`. It never contains the access token,
ID token, or refresh token.

## Wrap the Svelte app

The Svelte entry point is exported from `@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff/svelte`. Wrap the root layout
with `GateKeeperProvider`:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { GateKeeperProvider } from '@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff/svelte';

	let { data, children } = $props();
</script>

<GateKeeperProvider initialSession={data.gatekeeper}>
	{@render children()}
</GateKeeperProvider>
```

Add a same-origin refresh endpoint:

```ts
import { json } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { gatekeeper } from '$lib/server/gatekeeper';
import type { RequestHandler } from './$types';

export const GET: RequestHandler = async ({ locals, cookies }) => {
	const event = { locals, cookies };
	await gatekeeper.loadSession(event);
	return json(gatekeeper.getBrowserSession(event));
};
```

The provider calls that endpoint before `expiresAt`, so the browser context updates while the real
token refresh still happens on the server. Child components can read the context:

```ts
import { getGateKeeperContext } from '@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff/svelte';

const gatekeeper = getGateKeeperContext();
const user = $derived(gatekeeper.user);
```

## Update the signed-in user profile

The package wraps `PUT /me/profile` with typed camelCase inputs:

```ts
const profile = await gatekeeper.updateMyProfileForEvent(event, {
	displayName: 'Ada Lovelace',
	firstName: 'Ada',
	lastName: 'Lovelace'
});
```

When the update succeeds, the helper updates the request-local session and rewrites the encrypted
cookie so later reads see the new profile data.

Only profile names are updated by this helper. Email changes, password changes, and account
recovery remain separate GateKeeper flows.

## Use the default MFA component

`MfaConfiguration` renders a complete MFA settings UI for the signed-in user. It can list methods,
choose the preferred method, enroll and confirm TOTP, register passkeys, rename methods, and remove
methods.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { MfaConfiguration } from '@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff/svelte';

	let { data } = $props();
</script>

<MfaConfiguration
	initialMfa={data.mfa}
	classes={{
		section: 'settings-section',
		primaryButton: 'button-primary',
		dangerButton: 'button-danger'
	}}
/>
```

The component is intentionally styleable instead of opinionated. Consumers can target the default
`gk-mfa__*` classes or pass a `classes` map. Use `basePath` when your app exposes MFA proxy
endpoints somewhere other than `/api/mfa`.

The example frontend includes a styled wrapper at
`frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/lib/components/gatekeeper/styled-mfa-configuration.svelte`.

## Build custom MFA UI

Developers who need a fully custom component can use the same headless API:

```ts
import { createMfaClient } from '@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff/svelte';

const mfa = createMfaClient({ basePath: '/api/mfa' });

const state = await mfa.listMethods();
await mfa.setPreferredMethod('webauthn');
const totp = await mfa.startTotpEnrollment();
await mfa.confirmTotpEnrollment('123456');
await mfa.addPasskey('YubiKey 5C');
```

The helper talks to same-origin BFF endpoints. Those endpoints should proxy to GateKeeper with
server-side `idpFetch()`, so the browser never receives the access token directly.

The example frontend includes both default-component usage at `/account/security` and a headless
example at `/account/security/headless-example`.

## Example routes in the frontend

The current frontend demonstrates the package in these places:

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/hooks.server.ts` | Loads and refreshes sessions for every request. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/+layout.server.ts` | Exposes browser-safe session data. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/+layout.svelte` | Wraps the app with `GateKeeperProvider`. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/api/gatekeeper/session/+server.ts` | Refresh endpoint used by the provider. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/api/me/profile/+server.ts` | Profile update endpoint. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/api/mfa/**/+server.ts` | MFA proxy endpoints used by the component/headless client. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/account/security/+page.svelte` | Styled default MFA component usage. |
| `frontends/sveltekit-bff/src/routes/account/security/headless-example/+page.svelte` | Custom MFA UI built from the headless API. |
